14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"guide":{"date":"September 17, 1862","place":"The center of the Antietam battlefield east of Sharpsburg, Maryland, from the Roulette farm south across the ridge to the Sunken Road between the Hagerstown Turnpike and the Roulette farm lane","result":"Beginning at about 9:30 a.m., Brig. Gen. William H. French sent three brigade lines against Maj. Gen. D. H. Hill’s infantry in the Sunken Road. The road’s banks and piled fence rails protected the Confederates, who waited until the Federal lines crossed the ridge before firing at close range. Weber’s, Morris’s, and Kimball’s brigades were each stopped, and French’s division could not break the position before Israel Richardson’s division arrived. French reported 1,817 casualties; the episode uses 1,760. The first phase therefore ended with Hill’s line intact but under continuing pressure and with reinforcements approaching both sides","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"About 9:15 to 10:30 a.m. in the Confederate center","note":"The episode follows French from the East Woods through three failed brigade attacks and ends before Richardson’s division begins the next phase of the Sunken Road fight."},{"label":"French’s decision","value":"Turn south toward the only visible Confederate line","note":"Sedgwick had disappeared into the West Woods without leaving French a guide. French saw Greene near the Dunker Church and Confederate skirmishers farther south, then moved toward the Roulette farm."},{"label":"Sunken Road","value":"A farm lane worn several feet below the surrounding fields","note":"Wagon traffic and erosion created a natural infantry position. Confederate troops strengthened it by piling fence rails along the bank."},{"label":"Location of the defended sector","value":"About 600 yards south of the Dunker Church","note":"The episode traces the road east from the Hagerstown Turnpike to its bend at the Roulette farm lane and then southeast toward the Boonsboro Turnpike."},{"label":"Federal strength","value":"About 5,700 men in French’s division","note":"The division had only recently been assembled and attacked in three brigade lines under Max Weber, Dwight Morris, and Nathan Kimball."},{"label":"Confederate strength","value":"About 2,500 in the episode; about 2,200 in current NPS interpretation","note":"The difference reflects varying estimates of the effectives from Rodes’s and George B. Anderson’s brigades and the battered remnants extending the line west."},{"label":"Hill’s available brigades","value":"Two relatively intact brigades and remnants of two others","note":"Rodes and George B. Anderson held most of the road. The surviving portions of Colquitt’s brigade and Garland’s brigade under Duncan McRae extended the position toward the Hagerstown Turnpike."},{"label":"Rodes’s line","value":"About 750 Alabamians in five regiments in the episode","note":"From the road’s bend westward, the episode places the 6th, 5th, 3rd, 12th, and 26th Alabama. Rodes’s official report gives fewer than 800 effectives at the beginning of this fight."},{"label":"George B. Anderson’s line","value":"About 1,150 North Carolinians in four regiments","note":"The episode lists the 2nd, 14th, 4th, and 30th North Carolina from left to right in the deepest part of the road."},{"label":"First Federal wave","value":"About 1,700 men under Brig. Gen. Max Weber","note":"The 1st Delaware, 5th Maryland, and 4th New York advanced first. The slower pace of the large 5th Maryland color bearer caused the brigade line to bow inward."},{"label":"Confederate fire discipline","value":"Fire withheld until the Federals were about 80 yards away","note":"Officers kept the defenders down behind the road bank and fence rails until Weber’s line crossed the ridge and began descending the open slope."},{"label":"Weber’s repulse","value":"As many as 500 casualties within about five minutes in the episode","note":"Weber was severely wounded, all nine members of the 1st Delaware color guard fell, and the brigade stopped short of the road."},{"label":"1st Delaware","value":"286 of 635 men killed or wounded in less than five minutes","note":"This episode figure comes from Lt. Charles B. Tanner’s recollection and is also used in the National Park Service Bloody Lane trail interpretation."},{"label":"Tanner’s Medal of Honor action","value":"Recovered the 1st Delaware colors after three wounds","note":"Tanner reached the fallen flag, suffered a shattered arm and two additional wounds, and carried the colors back. He later received the Medal of Honor."},{"label":"Second Federal wave","value":"About 1,000 men under Col. Dwight Morris","note":"The 108th New York, 130th Pennsylvania, and 14th Connecticut were all new regiments entering combat for the first time."},{"label":"Friendly fire","value":"14th Connecticut fired into the rear of 1st Delaware","note":"Morris’s advancing green troops encountered survivors of Weber’s line amid heavy fire and confusion near the crest."},{"label":"Third Federal wave","value":"Kimball’s brigade shifted east and attacked","note":"The 14th Indiana, 8th Ohio, and 7th Virginia (later 7th West Virginia) were combat veterans; the 132nd Pennsylvania had only recently entered service."},{"label":"Roulette farm beehives","value":"A Confederate shell scattered bees among the 132nd Pennsylvania","note":"The regiment broke briefly in the orchard, rallied, and continued toward the Sunken Road. The NPS records William Roulette’s later compensation claim for damaged beehives and other property."},{"label":"Richard A. Oakford","value":"Killed while leading the 132nd Pennsylvania","note":"The automated transcript renders his surname as “Oakley.” Kimball’s official report identifies Col. Richard A. Oakford and states that he was killed while leading his regiment."},{"label":"French’s total loss","value":"1,817 officially reported; 1,760 in the episode","note":"French reported 1,614 killed or wounded and 203 missing. The nearby totals are retained rather than treated as interchangeable."},{"label":"Why the attacks stopped","value":"Open approach, protected defenders, and successive commitment","note":"Each Federal brigade crossed the ridge separately into close-range fire. Survivors used the reverse slope for cover while the next formation went forward."},{"label":"Reinforcements approaching","value":"Richardson for the United States; Richard H. Anderson for the Confederacy","note":"The episode closes before either formation’s role in the second phase is described."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“The Long Road to Antietam” by Richard Slotkin","note":"The full subtitle is “How the Civil War Became a Revolution.” The episode recommends it for its combined military and political account of the Maryland Campaign and emancipation."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Dunker Church, George B. Anderson, Robert Rodes, Richard A. Oakford, Roulette farm, and The Gleam of Bayonets","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “Dunkirk Church,” “Rhodes,” “Oakley,” “roulette,” and “In the Gleam of Bayonets.” This guide uses the historical names and titles."}],"maps":[{"title":"French turns toward the Confederate center","caption":"French reached the East Woods without orders or a guide from Sumner. He moved south through the Roulette farm toward the Sunken Road, where Rodes and George B. Anderson held the main Confederate position and battered remnants extended west to the Hagerstown Turnpike. Richard H. Anderson’s division approached from the south. Routes and positions are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield Bloody Lane Trail, Official Reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep196-french-approach.svg","alt":"Schematic map of French’s division moving south from the Roulette farm toward Confederate brigades in the Sunken Road."},{"title":"French’s three brigade attacks","caption":"Weber, Morris, and Kimball attacked in sequence across the ridge north of the Sunken Road. The protected defenders waited until each Federal line came into close range. Weber was wounded, Morris’s green regiments were checked, and Oakford was killed with Kimball’s 132nd Pennsylvania before the survivors fell back behind the ridge. Attacks and positions are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield Bloody Lane Trail stops 3–6, French’s and Kimball’s Official Reports, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep196-sunken-road-waves.svg","alt":"Schematic tactical diagram showing Weber, Morris, and Kimball attacking the Sunken Road in three successive waves."},{"title":"Confederate dead in the Sunken Road","caption":"Alexander Gardner photographed dead Confederate soldiers in the road two days after the battle. The image records the aftermath, not the fighting described in the episode.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep196-bloody-lane.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of dead Confederate soldiers in the Sunken Road at Antietam, with a Union soldier standing above the bank."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:12","audioTime":72,"title":"The northern attacks are over","detail":"Sedgwick has been routed and Greene has lost his foothold near the Dunker Church, shifting the fighting toward Lee’s center."},{"time":"1:30","audioTime":90,"title":"The Sunken Road becomes the new focus","detail":"D. H. Hill’s infantry holds a farm lane in the center of the Confederate line."},{"time":"2:15","audioTime":135,"title":"French reaches the East Woods without orders","detail":"Sumner has left no staff officer to direct French, and Sedgwick is already out of sight in the West Woods."},{"time":"2:56","audioTime":176,"title":"French surveys the visible Federal line","detail":"Greene’s troops near the Dunker Church are the only Federal formation he can see."},{"time":"3:09","audioTime":189,"title":"French moves south","detail":"He intends to form on Greene’s left and advances toward the Roulette farm instead of following Sedgwick."},{"time":"3:22","audioTime":202,"title":"The Sunken Road is located","detail":"The episode traces the defended lane east from the Hagerstown Turnpike and then southeast toward the Boonsboro Turnpike."},{"time":"4:14","audioTime":254,"title":"A farm lane provides natural cover","detail":"Erosion and wagon traffic have worn parts of the road several feet below the adjoining fields."},{"time":"4:33","audioTime":273,"title":"Lee and Hill see French approaching","detail":"The two generals ride along the center and recognize that the Federal column is moving toward Hill’s line."},{"time":"4:48","audioTime":288,"title":"John B. Gordon answers Lee","detail":"The 6th Alabama colonel assures Lee that his regiment will remain in position."},{"time":"5:03","audioTime":303,"title":"The senior Confederates move to higher ground","detail":"Lee and Hill join Longstreet on a ridge near the Hagerstown Turnpike to observe the Federal advance."},{"time":"5:30","audioTime":330,"title":"A Federal shell kills Hill’s horse","detail":"The shell severs the animal’s front legs while Hill remains mounted; Hill escapes injury."},{"time":"6:11","audioTime":371,"title":"French reaches the Roulette farm","detail":"His three brigades drive Confederate skirmishers away from the farm buildings."},{"time":"6:30","audioTime":390,"title":"French receives an urgent attack order","detail":"News of Sedgwick’s disaster finally reaches him, and he is told to attack quickly to reduce the pressure in the West Woods."},{"time":"7:06","audioTime":426,"title":"The division advances in three lines","detail":"French moves south over rising ground toward Confederates hidden beyond the crest."},{"time":"7:26","audioTime":446,"title":"Hill’s strength is reduced","detail":"Three of his five brigades have already fought on the northern field, leaving perhaps 2,500 men in the episode’s estimate."},{"time":"8:17","audioTime":497,"title":"Richard H. Anderson approaches","detail":"Lee sends another Confederate division toward the center while French closes on the road."},{"time":"8:25","audioTime":505,"title":"George B. Anderson holds the deepest road cut","detail":"His four North Carolina regiments occupy the eastern portion of the defended sector."},{"time":"8:51","audioTime":531,"title":"Rodes holds the bend","detail":"Five Alabama regiments extend west from the junction with the Roulette farm lane."},{"time":"9:48","audioTime":588,"title":"Battered brigades extend Hill’s left","detail":"The remnants under Alfred Colquitt and Duncan McRae connect Rodes with the Hagerstown Turnpike."},{"time":"11:12","audioTime":672,"title":"Gordon explains his fire plan","detail":"He orders the 6th Alabama to remain down and hold its fire until the Federals are at close range."},{"time":"13:30","audioTime":810,"title":"Tanner’s account records the 1st Delaware’s losses","detail":"He recalls 286 of 635 men and eight of ten company commanders killed or wounded in less than five minutes."},{"time":"15:30","audioTime":930,"title":"French’s division is described","detail":"The recently assembled command contains veteran units, new regiments, and three brigades that will attack separately."},{"time":"16:07","audioTime":967,"title":"Weber begins the first attack","detail":"The 1st Delaware, 5th Maryland, and 4th New York form the first Federal line."},{"time":"16:44","audioTime":1004,"title":"Weber’s line bows inward","detail":"The 5th Maryland falls behind the two flank regiments during the advance."},{"time":"16:55","audioTime":1015,"title":"The defenders continue to wait","detail":"Confederate officers prevent their men from firing as the Federal line crosses the ridge."},{"time":"17:04","audioTime":1024,"title":"The Sunken Road erupts at close range","detail":"At about eighty yards, the Confederate line opens a coordinated volley on Weber’s exposed brigade."},{"time":"17:21","audioTime":1041,"title":"Weber’s brigade is stopped","detail":"The episode estimates as many as 500 Federal casualties in five minutes, including the severely wounded Weber."},{"time":"17:32","audioTime":1052,"title":"The 1st Delaware color guard falls","detail":"All nine members are down and the regiment’s colors remain near the Confederate position."},{"time":"17:46","audioTime":1066,"title":"Charles Tanner saves the colors","detail":"Tanner retrieves the 1st Delaware flag, receives three wounds, and later earns the Medal of Honor."},{"time":"17:53","audioTime":1073,"title":"Morris sends in the second brigade","detail":"About 1,000 men in three new regiments follow Weber toward the ridge."},{"time":"18:20","audioTime":1100,"title":"Friendly fire adds to the confusion","detail":"Members of the 14th Connecticut fire into the rear of the 1st Delaware."},{"time":"18:28","audioTime":1108,"title":"Morris can advance no farther","detail":"His brigade and Weber’s survivors withdraw behind the ridge and return fire from cover."},{"time":"18:45","audioTime":1125,"title":"Samuel Fisk describes a first battle","detail":"The 14th Connecticut captain records disordered commands, retreating troops, and inexperienced soldiers firing under stress."},{"time":"19:33","audioTime":1173,"title":"Confederates counterattack from the road","detail":"Small impromptu advances expose some defenders but help push Morris’s line back behind the crest."},{"time":"19:54","audioTime":1194,"title":"Kimball receives the third attack","detail":"French commits his last brigade after the first two have been repulsed."},{"time":"20:40","audioTime":1240,"title":"Kimball’s mixed experience is identified","detail":"Three regiments are veterans, while the 132nd Pennsylvania has only recently entered service."},{"time":"21:03","audioTime":1263,"title":"A shell breaks beehives in the orchard","detail":"The 132nd Pennsylvania scatters briefly among the bees at the Roulette farm before rallying."},{"time":"21:51","audioTime":1311,"title":"Kimball passes the first two brigades","detail":"His line shifts east and advances beyond Weber’s and Morris’s men on the reverse slope."},{"time":"22:14","audioTime":1334,"title":"Oakford is killed","detail":"The 132nd Pennsylvania’s colonel falls in the first Confederate volley that strikes Kimball’s line."},{"time":"22:44","audioTime":1364,"title":"Kimball’s veterans call it systematic killing","detail":"The third brigade cannot pass the same protected position that stopped Weber and Morris."},{"time":"23:12","audioTime":1392,"title":"French’s offensive power is exhausted","detail":"Kimball falls back to regroup, leaving all three Federal brigades short of the Sunken Road."},{"time":"23:23","audioTime":1403,"title":"The first phase ends","detail":"The episode gives French 1,760 casualties and notes that fresh formations are approaching both sides."},{"time":"23:43","audioTime":1423,"title":"The book recommendation begins","detail":"The hosts recommend Richard Slotkin’s “The Long Road to Antietam.”"},{"time":"24:25","audioTime":1465,"title":"The campaign is connected to emancipation","detail":"The recommendation emphasizes how the military result at Antietam enabled Lincoln to issue the preliminary Emancipation Proclamation."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Ordered II Corps into the battle; the episode begins after its divisions had become separated during the approach"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner","command":"II Corps","subordinates":"Led Sedgwick away without leaving French a guide, then sent orders for French to press the Confederate center"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. William H. French","command":"3rd Division, II Corps","subordinates":"Directed about 5,700 men in three brigade attacks from the Roulette farm toward the Sunken Road"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Max Weber","command":"1st Brigade, French’s division","subordinates":"Led the 1st Delaware, 5th Maryland, and 4th New York in the first assault; severely wounded"},{"commander":"Col. Dwight Morris","command":"2nd Brigade, French’s division","subordinates":"Led the new 108th New York, 130th Pennsylvania, and 14th Connecticut in the second assault"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Nathan Kimball","command":"3rd Brigade, French’s division","subordinates":"Shifted east with the 14th Indiana, 8th Ohio, 7th Virginia (later 7th West Virginia), and 132nd Pennsylvania for the third assault"},{"commander":"Col. Richard A. Oakford","command":"132nd Pennsylvania, Kimball’s brigade","subordinates":"Rallied the new regiment after the beehive incident and was killed while leading it near the ridge"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#963b31","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Inspected Hill’s line as French approached and directed Richard H. Anderson’s division toward the center"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Confederate right wing","subordinates":"Observed the approach with Lee and Hill from high ground near the Hagerstown Turnpike"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. D. H. Hill","command":"Hill’s division and the Sunken Road defense","subordinates":"Placed the center along the farm road with Rodes and George B. Anderson holding its strongest sections"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Robert E. Rodes","command":"Rodes’s brigade, Hill’s division","subordinates":"Held five Alabama regiments west of the road’s bend and stopped part of French’s attack"},{"commander":"Col. John B. Gordon","command":"6th Alabama, Rodes’s brigade","subordinates":"Held the junction with George B. Anderson’s line and ordered his regiment to withhold fire until close range"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George B. Anderson","command":"Anderson’s brigade, Hill’s division","subordinates":"Held four North Carolina regiments in the deepest eastern section of the Sunken Road; mortally wounded during the fighting"},{"commander":"Col. Alfred H. Colquitt and Col. Duncan K. McRae","command":"Remnants on Hill’s left","subordinates":"Extended the Confederate position west from Rodes toward the Hagerstown Turnpike after earlier fighting"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Richard H. Anderson","command":"Anderson’s division, Longstreet’s wing","subordinates":"Approached from the south and did not participate in the three assaults covered by this episode"}]}],"people":[{"name":"William H. French","role":"Commander of the II Corps division that made the first three brigade attacks on the Sunken Road","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep196-william-french.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-stop-3.htm"},{"name":"Max Weber","role":"Commander of French’s first brigade line, severely wounded during the opening assault","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=54"},{"name":"Dwight Morris","role":"Colonel who led French’s three new regiments in the second assault","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=54"},{"name":"Nathan Kimball","role":"Commander of French’s third brigade line and author of an official report on its attack","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=126"},{"name":"Richard A. Oakford","role":"Colonel of the 132nd Pennsylvania, killed while leading the regiment in Kimball’s attack","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=126"},{"name":"Charles B. Tanner","role":"1st Delaware lieutenant who recovered the regiment’s colors after receiving three wounds and later received the Medal of Honor","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-stop-6.htm"},{"name":"Samuel W. Fiske","role":"14th Connecticut captain whose next-day letter described the confusion experienced by the new regiment","side":"United States"},{"name":"D. H. Hill","role":"Division commander responsible for the Confederate center and the infantry holding the Sunken Road","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=32"},{"name":"Robert E. Rodes","role":"Brigade commander whose Alabamians held the western part of the main Sunken Road position","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=74"},{"name":"George B. Anderson","role":"North Carolina brigade commander in the deepest portion of the road, mortally wounded in the Sunken Road fighting","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-stop-5.htm"},{"name":"John B. Gordon","role":"6th Alabama colonel who controlled his regiment’s close-range opening fire at the bend in the road","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-stop-5.htm"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army commander who inspected Hill’s position and sent reinforcements toward the Confederate center","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-stop-5.htm"}],"losses":[{"name":"Brig. Gen. Max Weber","side":"United States","role":"Commander of 1st Brigade, French’s division","outcome":"Severely wounded while leading the first assault. Col. John W. Andrews of the 1st Delaware assumed brigade command and ordered the shattered line to fall back."},{"name":"Col. Richard A. Oakford","side":"United States","role":"Commander of the 132nd Pennsylvania","outcome":"Killed while leading his new regiment in Kimball’s attack. His surname is incorrectly rendered as “Oakley” in the automated transcript."},{"name":"Brig. Gen. George B. Anderson","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander of Anderson’s North Carolina brigade","outcome":"Wounded in the ankle during the Sunken Road fighting. The wound became infected, and he died in Raleigh, North Carolina, on October 16, 1862."},{"name":"Lt. Charles B. Tanner","side":"United States","role":"1st Delaware Infantry","outcome":"Wounded three times while recovering the regimental colors after the color guard fell. He survived and later received the Medal of Honor."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep196-french-approach.svg","alt":"Schematic map of French’s approach from the Roulette farm to the Sunken Road.","caption":"French turned south after losing contact with Sumner and Sedgwick, bringing his division against Hill’s center.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, Official Reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript."},{"image":"/images/ep196-sunken-road-waves.svg","alt":"Schematic diagram of French’s three successive attacks against the Sunken Road.","caption":"The three brigade lines crossed the same ridge in sequence and were each stopped by close-range fire from the protected road.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, French’s and Kimball’s Official Reports, and episode transcript."},{"image":"/images/ep196-william-french.jpg","alt":"Portrait of United States brigadier general William Henry French in uniform.","caption":"William H. French commanded the II Corps division that opened the Federal attack on the Sunken Road.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, National Park Service."},{"image":"/images/ep196-bloody-lane.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of dead Confederate soldiers in the Sunken Road after Antietam.","caption":"Gardner photographed the Sunken Road on September 19, 1862, after the deadliest fighting there had ended.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 196 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/05/21/civil-war-podcast-episode-196/"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Bloody Lane Trail overview","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Roulette Farm","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-stop-2.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: uphill to the Sunken Road","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-stop-3.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Fire!","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-stop-4.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Sunken Road to Bloody Lane","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-stop-5.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Charles Tanner’s Medal of Honor action","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-stop-6.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Bloody Lane table of organization","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/bloody-lane-trail-table-of-organization.htm"},{"label":"Official Records, Series I, Volume XIX, Part I","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/027"},{"label":"Primary source: William H. French’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=54"},{"label":"Primary source: Nathan Kimball’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=126"},{"label":"Primary source: D. H. Hill’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=32"},{"label":"Primary source: Robert E. Rodes’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=74"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Atlas of the battlefield of Antietam","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/map05000006/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Gardner photograph of the Sunken Road","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2014646934/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Confederate dead in the Sunken Road",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep196-bloody-lane.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Confederate dead in the Sunken Road at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep196-bloody-lane.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of dead Confederate soldiers in the Sunken Road at Antietam, with a Union soldier standing above the bank.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Confederate dead in the Sunken Road"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Alexander Gardner photographed dead Confederate soldiers in the road two days after the battle. The image records the aftermath, not the fighting described in the episode."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep196-bloody-lane.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Ordered II Corps into the battle; the episode begins after its divisions had become separated during the approach"}]]}],["$","div","II Corps-Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"II Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Led Sedgwick away without leaving French a guide, then sent orders for French to press the Confederate center"}]]}],["$","div","3rd Division, II Corps-Brig. Gen. William H. French",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"3rd Division, II Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. William H. French"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Directed about 5,700 men in three brigade attacks from the Roulette farm toward the Sunken Road"}]]}],["$","div","1st Brigade, French’s division-Brig. Gen. Max Weber",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"1st Brigade, French’s division"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Max Weber"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Led the 1st Delaware, 5th Maryland, and 4th New York in the first assault; severely wounded"}]]}],["$","div","2nd Brigade, French’s division-Col. Dwight Morris",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"2nd Brigade, French’s division"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Dwight Morris"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Led the new 108th New York, 130th Pennsylvania, and 14th Connecticut in the second assault"}]]}],["$","div","3rd Brigade, French’s division-Brig. Gen. Nathan Kimball",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"3rd Brigade, French’s division"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Nathan Kimball"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Shifted east with the 14th Indiana, 8th Ohio, 7th Virginia (later 7th West Virginia), and 132nd Pennsylvania for the third assault"}]]}],["$","div","132nd Pennsylvania, Kimball’s brigade-Col. Richard A. Oakford",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"132nd Pennsylvania, Kimball’s brigade"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Richard A. Oakford"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Rallied the new regiment after the beehive incident and was killed while leading it near the ridge"}]]}]]]}],"$L1e"]}],"$L1f"]]]}]
17:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"losses","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Important leaders killed or wounded"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"loss-list","children":[["$","article","Brig. Gen. Max Weber",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Max Weber"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander of 1st Brigade, French’s division","."]}]," ","Severely wounded while leading the first assault. Col. John W. Andrews of the 1st Delaware assumed brigade command and ordered the shattered line to fall back."]}]]}],["$","article","Col. Richard A. Oakford",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Col. Richard A. Oakford"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander of the 132nd Pennsylvania","."]}]," ","Killed while leading his new regiment in Kimball’s attack. His surname is incorrectly rendered as “Oakley” in the automated transcript."]}]]}],["$","article","Brig. Gen. George B. Anderson",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side confederate","children":"Confederacy"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. George B. Anderson"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander of Anderson’s North Carolina brigade","."]}]," ","Wounded in the ankle during the Sunken Road fighting. The wound became infected, and he died in Raleigh, North Carolina, on October 16, 1862."]}]]}],["$","article","Lt. Charles B. Tanner",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Lt. Charles B. Tanner"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["1st Delaware Infantry","."]}]," ","Wounded three times while recovering the regimental colors after the color guard fell. He survived and later received the Medal of Honor."]}]]}]]}]]}]
18:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L20","/images/ep196-william-french.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep196-william-french.jpg","alt":"Portrait of United States brigadier general William Henry French in uniform.","caption":"William H. French commanded the II Corps division that opened the Federal attack on the Sunken Road.","name":"Portrait of United States brigadier general William Henry French in uniform.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Antietam National Battlefield, National Park Service."}],["$","$L20","/images/ep196-bloody-lane.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep196-bloody-lane.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of dead Confederate soldiers in the Sunken Road after Antietam.","caption":"Gardner photographed the Sunken Road on September 19, 1862, after the deadliest fighting there had ended.","name":"Alexander Gardner photograph of dead Confederate soldiers in the Sunken Road after Antietam.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."}]]}]]}]
